    "abstract": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) is a complex, multifactorial metabolic disorder characterized by chronic hyperglycemia, insulin resistance, and progressive pancreatic \u03b2-cell dysfunction. Beyond metabolic dysregulation, increasing evidence highlights a central role for oxidative stress and chronic low-grade inflammation in the onset and progression of pancreatic damage, ultimately leading to \u03b2-cell failure. These interconnected mechanisms support the exploration of therapeutic approaches capable of modulating metabolic, inflammatory, and oxidative stress pathways, including bioactive compounds of natural origin. In this context, we investigated whether stem and root decoction extracts of Cucumis prophetarum L. could improve pancreatic function and systemic metabolic alterations in a rat model of T2DM. Diabetes was induced by a combination of high-fat diet and a single intraperitoneal injection of streptozotocin. Following confirmation of diabetes induction, animals were treated by oral gavage with the decoction extracts for 14 days.\n\nTreatment with both stem and root extracts attenuated diabetes-induced body weight loss and preserved metabolically active tissue mass, suggesting an improvement in systemic metabolic status. Moreover, the extracts significantly enhanced insulin sensitivity and glucose tolerance, as demonstrated by the Insulin Tolerance Test (ITT) and Intraperitoneal Glucose Tolerance Test (IPGTT). At the pancreatic level, treatment restored pancreatic function and markedly reduced oxidative stress and inflammation. In addition, the extracts modulated key cellular stress pathways, including autophagy and apoptosis, indicating a protective effect against \u03b2-cell damage.\n\nOverall, these findings suggest that Cucumis prophetarum L. may represent a potential natural candidate for the treatment of T2DM, with possible implications for the development of novel therapeutic strategies targeting T2DM-related metabolic and pancreatic dysfunction.",
    "biography": "Maria Ventriglia is a PhD student in Biomolecular Sciences at the University of Campania \u201cL. Vanvitelli\u201d (DiSTABiF). She is actively involved in research activities at the General Physiology Laboratory, where her work focuses on mitochondrial quality control mechanisms regulating metabolic homeostasis and disease. She has co-authored several peer-reviewed articles published in international journals indexed in Scopus and/or Web of Science and has contributed to various presentations at national and international conferences.",
    "views": 510,
    "downloads": 115,
    "citations": 2,
    "doi": "10.scientra/cucumis-prophetarum-extracts-metabolic-pancreatic-dysfunction"
