    "publicationDate": "2026-06-20",
    "abstract": "Opioids are still considered mainstay for cardiac surgical patients. However, due to the need reduce opioid use and optimize recovery, there has been a significant push to implement multimodal techniques to reduce the opioid use in cardiac surgery. Many facets of multimodal analgesia have been incorporated into the goals of Enhanced Recovery Models/Clinical Pathways. Here we will review multiple components of multimodal analgesia for a successful ERACS model and reduce opioid use in the cardiac surgical patient population.\n\nObjectives:\n\nReview the current condition on the opioid use in cardiac surgical patient and briefly review components of ERACS (Enhanced Recovery after Cardiac Surgery) and its utility in cardiac surgery\n\nDiscuss pharmacological management and review current literature\n\nReview the application of regional techniques for cardiac and cardiac-related procedures",
    "views": 500,
    "downloads": 100,
    "citations": 2,
    "doi": "10.scientra/eracs-and-multimodal-analgesia-is-this-is-the-answer-to-the-opioid-crisis-in-cardiac-patients",
    "biography": "Himani Bhatt has received her training in some of the finest institutions having completed her residency training at St. Lukes-Roosevelt Hospital Columbia University in New York City and then further continuing and successfully completing her cardiothoracic fellowship at Duke University Heart Center in Durham, NC.  She holds active board certifications in Anesthesiology and Advanced perioperative TEE by the National Board of Echocardiography (NBE) since 2010.  She is currently an associate professor of Anesthesiology, Perioperative and Pain Medicine at the Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai and serves as the Director of Cardiothoracic Anesthesiology at Mount Sinai Morningside Medical Center.  In addition, she has mentored multiple residents, fellows and faculty, presented at multiple national and international conferences, and has published over fourty publications in peer-reviewed journals."
